To determine the input impedance at each terminal, deactivate the other input source (replace the voltage source by a short circuit). <strong>Input impedance seen at \(V_{1}\):</strong><br/>With \(V_{2}=0\), \[V_{+}=0.\] For an ideal op-amp with negative feedback, \[V_{-}=V_{+}=0.\] Hence, the source connected to \(V_{1}\) sees only \[R_{1}=47\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Therefore, \[Z_{in1}=47\,\text{k}\Omega.\] <strong>Input impedance seen at \(V_{2}\):</strong><br/>With \(V_{1}=0\), the non-inverting input draws no current. Hence, the current from \(V_{2}\) flows through \[R_{1}\;\text{and}\;R_{2}\] in series. Therefore, \[Z_{in2}=R_{1}+R_{2}
=47+470
=517\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Thus, \[\boxed{\text{(c) }47\,\text{k}\Omega\ \text{and}\ 517\,\text{k}\Omega}\]

The given circuit is a first-order inverting active low-pass filter. The feedback impedance is \[Z_{f}=R_{2}\parallel\frac{1}{sC_{1}}
=\frac{R_{2}}{1+sR_{2}C_{1}}.\] Hence, the transfer function is \[\frac{V_{o}}{V_{i}}
=-\frac{Z_{f}}{R_{1}}
=
-\frac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}
\cdot
\frac{1}{1+sR_{2}C_{1}}.\] The dc gain is \[|A_{v}|=\frac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}
=\frac{159}{15.9}=10.\] Therefore, \[20\log_{10}(10)=20\,\text{dB}.\] The \(3\,\text{dB}\) cutoff frequency is \[f_{c}
=\frac{1}{2\pi R_{2}C_{1}}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(159\times10^{3})(1\times10^{-9})}
\approx1001\,\text{Hz}
\approx1.00\,\text{kHz}.\] Thus, \[\boxed{\text{(d) }20\,\text{dB},\;1.00\,\text{kHz}}\]

From the asymptotic Bode magnitude plot, the slope changes from \(0\) to \(+20\,\text{dB/decade}\) at \(\omega_z=0.1\,\text{rad/s}\) and changes back to \(0\) at \(\omega_p=0.5\,\text{rad/s}.\) For a lead network, \[G(s)=\frac{1+s/\omega_z}{1+s/\omega_p},
\qquad \omega_p&gt;\omega_z.\] The maximum phase lead occurs at the geometric mean of the zero and pole frequencies. \[\omega_m=\sqrt{\omega_z\omega_p}
=\sqrt{0.1\times0.5}
=\sqrt{0.05}
=\sqrt{\frac{1}{20}}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{20}}\,\text{rad/s}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{20}}}\]

We evaluate the total workload requirements by finding the work rate of each individual category of worker. Let the total work to build the wall be represented as \(W\) units. <strong>Step 1: Calculate the work rates per worker category per day</strong><br/><ul><li>Skilled Workers: 5 workers take 20 days \(\implies 5 \times 20 = 100\) skilled worker-days. \[\text{Rate of 1 skilled worker } (R_k) = \dfrac{1}{100} W/\text{day}\]</li><li>Semi-Skilled Workers: 8 workers take 25 days \(\implies 8 \times 25 = 200\) semi-skilled worker-days. \[\text{Rate of 1 semi-skilled worker } (R_m) = \dfrac{1}{200} W/\text{day}\]</li><li>Unskilled Workers: 10 workers take 30 days \(\implies 10 \times 30 = 300\) unskilled worker-days. \[\text{Rate of 1 unskilled worker } (R_n) = \dfrac{1}{300} W/\text{day}\]</li></ul> <strong>Step 2: Calculate the combined work rate of the composite team</strong><br/>The combined team consists of 2 skilled, 6 semi-skilled, and 5 unskilled workers: \[\begin{aligned}
\text{Total Team Rate } (R_{\text{team}}) &amp;= 2R_k + 6R_m + 5R_n \\
&amp;= 2\left(\dfrac{1}{100}\right) + 6\left(\dfrac{1}{200}\right) + 5\left(\dfrac{1}{300}\right) \\
&amp;= \dfrac{2}{100} + \dfrac{3}{100} + \dfrac{1}{60} \\
&amp;= \dfrac{5}{100} + \dfrac{1}{60} = \dfrac{1}{20} + \dfrac{1}{60}
\end{aligned}\] Find a common denominator to add the fractions: \[R_{\text{team}} = \dfrac{3 + 1}{60} = \dfrac{4}{60} = \dfrac{1}{15} W/\text{day}\] <strong>Step 3: Calculate the total time required</strong><br/>\[\text{Time Required} = \dfrac{W}{R_{\text{team}}} = \dfrac{1}{\frac{1}{15}} = 15 \text{ days}\] \[\boxed{\text{D: 15 days}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Composite Work Group Rates</strong>: Convert group labor periods into independent worker-day rate allocations. The overall capacity of a mixed workforce group is the direct linear sum of those individual component capacities.</li></ul></div>
